        <fr:title text="1.1 Basic Notions">1.1 Basic Notions</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kolmogorov_spaceThe_Kolmogorov_quotient" type="external">Kolmogorov Quotients</fr:link> are always <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T_0]]></fr:tex> (or Kolmogorov spaces). Kolmogorov spaces are a reflective subcategory of topological spaces, the Kolmogorov quotient is the reflector.</html:li>
        <html:li>Metrizable spaces have countable neighbourhood bases of points: Take the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U_{\varepsilon } (x)]]></fr:tex> with rational <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\varepsilon ]]></fr:tex>.</html:li>
        <html:li>It has become clear to me now that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> is some kind of ruler and a reference structure for much of these developments. Perhaps just as <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex> was for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex>, through <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Z}]]></fr:tex> and further <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>. I'm stating a simple fact here, e.g., the codomain of a metric is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> (that's how we defined it), that's all. I never deeply internalised that the domain for a metric can't be anything else. Which leads to the natural question, is there a generalisation of a metric wrt. its codomain, which can have something else for its codomain, or, must have an abstract object <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex>, which must satisfy some properties, and so on.</html:li></html:ul>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
